    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our offensive engine is headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (76.20) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75), both of whom benefit from ideal situational setups. **Mickey Moniak** is positioned in the two-hole against Sasaki, where he exploits a significant platoon advantage (+14%) and the opposing pitcher's glacial delivery, which pushes his stolen base potential to a 100% efficiency probability. **Corbin Carroll** remains a core anchor, though we have shaved a bit off his ceiling due to the opponent\u2019s tendency to hold runners, dampening his elite speed profile slightly. Meanwhile, **James Wood** (73.06) continues to be an analytical darling; his position at the top of the lineup combined with a stellar .429 xwOBA makes him a high-floor play despite the neutral platoon matchup against Arrighetti.\n\nOn the bench, **Isaac Paredes** (49.35) came closest to cracking the starting nine, but his projection suffered due to a suboptimal matchup against Griffin\u2019s 3.69 xERA and a negative dynamic platoon adjustment (-9%) when batting left-handed. We ultimately prioritized the higher-upside profiles of **Josh Naylor** (72.36) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (71.14), both of whom capitalize on favorable matchups against Phillips and Melton, respectively. We are tempering expectations for **Seiya Suzuki** (51.88) today; while he enjoys a park factor boost, he is facing a sub-3.00 xERA arm in Kremer, making him a marginal play despite his offensive pedigree.\n\nRisk management remains a priority given the current standing. **Zach McKinstry** (55.46) and **Jose Caballero** (55.46) both occupy the lower-scoring spectrum of our starters due to the heavy penalty of batting ninth, though **Jose Caballero** is a distinct tactical play against the slow-moving McClanahan, where his presence on the basepaths is maximized. We are keeping a close eye on **Seiya Suzuki**\u2019s performance in his tough matchup, and should any weather delays impact the evening games, we have sufficient depth on the bench\u2014including **Henry Bolte** and **Edwin Arroyo**\u2014to pivot if our primary indicators shift post-first pitch.",
